WHAT IS DERIVED FROM IT

  The triangle layout. A triangle is stored as three float4, holding the first
  vertex and both edges rather than three loose vertices:

      element 0 = v0
      element 1 = v1 minus v0
      element 2 = v2 minus v0

  so the traversal reads a triangle as three vector loads instead of nine
  scalar ones, and the ray triangle test gets its edges already subtracted.
  This comes from The Forge's hybrid ray tracing sample, 09a_HybridRaytracing,
  which is present in tag v1.45 and has since been removed from their main
  branch.

  Two traversal choices from the same sample: testing an instance's own root
  box before entering it, and pushing a node's children so that one chosen
  child is tested before its siblings. Which child that is, is decided by this
  plugin's own build.

  In this plugin those appear in Shaders/SWRTTriangleExtractCS.usf,
  Shaders/SWRTTraceCS.usf, Shaders/SWRTBVHBoundsCS.usf and the triangle
  handling in Source/PrototypeSWRTShadows/Private/SWRTSoftwareTrace.cpp. The
  code is written for this plugin and is not a copy of theirs; what was taken
  is the layout and those two traversal decisions.

  The Forge is under the same licence as this plugin, so nothing further is
  needed beyond keeping this notice.


UNREAL ENGINE
-------------

This reference implementation is written for Unreal Engine. It calls engine 
rendering interfaces and includes engine shader headers, which carries no 
condition beyond the Engine licence those users already hold.

It contains no transcribed Unreal Engine source. Developers porting this work 
to other engines or custom renderers are free to replace these engine-specific 
interfaces with their own implementations under the Apache 2.0 terms of this project.

There are four places wherethis plugin has to agree with the engine, because a light 
from this plugin and an engine spot light are meant to be comparable side by side:

  which lights are drawn and how they fade   IsSWRTSpotLightVisibleInView
  which primitives cast shadows              SWRTCasterGather.cpp
  how a light's parameters are packed        FillLightParameters
  the shade itself                           SWRTSpotLightPS.usf

The first three are reimplemented from the same quantities, so the behaviour
matches while the code is this plugin's own. The fourth includes the engine's
DeferredLightingCommon.ush and calls GetDynamicLighting, which is calling an
interface rather than copying one.
